ry Portland cement concrete specimens were cured under water up to 28 days and after that they were cured in the room environment $\\left( {{23}^{0}\\mathrm{C}}\\right.$ and ${60} \\pm {10}\\%$ RH) until test.\n![0197448a-5ae2-7ee0-acc3-abe53a1a126a_53_528_1328_630_483_0.jpg](images/0197448a-5ae2-7ee0-acc3-abe53a1a126a_53_528_1328_630_483_0.jpg)\nFigure 3-6: OPC concrete specimens cured in lime saturated water curing tank.\nCapping was done at 26 days in accordance with ASTM C-617 (ASTM standard, 2012b). Forney Hi-cap high strength capping compound was used for capping of the cylinders. If any defects were found in capping then cylinder was recapped before the testing.\n### 3.6. Test procedure\n#### 3.6.1. Particle size distribution.\nSieve analysis was used to determine the proportion of particles of different sizes within a particular aggregate product. The test used a tower of interlocking sieves with apertures that decreased in size from top to bottom. Sieve analysis was conducted as per the AS 1289.3.6.1-2009 (Standard Australia, 2009).\n## Fine aggregate\nThe test sample(1kg)was dried to a constant weight at a temperature of ${110} \\pm {5}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and weighed.', 'title': '', 'score': 0.24, 'source_type': 'document', 'source': 'knowledge_base', 'metadata': {'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'extra': None, 'headings': [], 'strategy': 'semantic', 'page_number': [1], 'dual_vector_metadata': "{'general_text': '\\\\pm 8$ hours and marked with respect to batch and mix id no, then immediately returned to the curing room. During the stripping time extra care was taken to avoid any type of damage of the specimens. The geopolymer and OPC concrete samples were put in different curing environment conditions. ![0197448a-5ae2-7ee0-acc3-abe53a1a126a_53_543_303_605_460_0.jpg](images/0197448a-5ae2-7ee0-acc3-abe53a1a126a_53_543_303_605_460_0.jpg)\\n## Figure 3-5: Geopolymer concrete specimens cured at ambient condition.\\nGeopolymer concrete samples were cured at ambient condition $\\\\left( {{15} - {20}^{0}\\\\mathrm{C}}\\\\right.$ and ${60} \\\\pm {10}\\\\% \\\\mathrm{{RH}}$ ) until the test days in the designated laboratory curing room.\\nOrdinary Portland cement concrete specimens were cured under water up to 28 days and after that they were cured in the room environment $\\\\left( {{23}^{0}\\\\mathrm{C}}\\\\right.$ and ${60} \\\\pm {10}\\\\%$ RH) until test.\\n![0197448a-5ae2-7ee0-acc3-abe53a1a126a_53_528_1328_630_483_0.jpg](images/0197448a-5ae2-7ee0-acc3-abe53a1a126a_53_528_1328_630_483_0.jpg)\\nFigure 3-6: OPC concrete specimens cured in lime saturated water curing tank.\\nCapping was done at 26 days in accordance with ASTM C-617 (ASTM standard, 2012b). Forney Hi-cap high strength capping compound was used for capping of the cylinders. If any defects were found in capping then cylinder was recapped before the testing.\\n### 3.6. Test procedure\\n#### 3.6.1. Particle size distribution.\\nSieve analysis was used to determine the proportion of particles of different sizes within a particular aggregate product. The test used a tower of interlocking sieves with apertures that decreased in size from top to bottom. Sieve analysis was conducted as per the AS 1289.3.6.1-2009 (Standard Australia, 2009).\\n## Fine aggregate\\nThe test sample(1kg)was dried to a constant weight at a temperature of ${110} \\\\pm {5}^{ \\\\circ }\\\\mathrm{C}$ and weighed.', 'domain_text': '\\\\pm 8$ hours and marked with respect to batch and mix id no, then immediately returned to the curing room. During the stripping time extra care was taken to avoid any type of damage of the specimens. The geopolymer and OPC concrete samples were put in different curing environment conditions. ![0197448a-5ae2-7ee0-acc3-abe53a1a126a_53_543_303_605_460_0.jpg](images/0197448a-5ae2-7ee0-acc3-abe53a1a126a_53_543_303_605_460_0.jpg)\\n## Figure 3-5: Geopolymer concrete specimens cured at ambient condition.\\nGeopolymer concrete samples were cured at ambient condition $\\\\left( {{15} - {20}^{0}\\\\mathrm{C}}\\\\right.$ and ${60} \\\\pm {10}\\\\% \\\\mathrm{{RH}}$ ) until the test days in the designated laboratory curing room.\\nOrdinary Portland cement concrete specimens were cured under water up to 28 days and after that they were cured in the room environment $\\\\left( {{23}^{0}\\\\mathrm{C}}\\\\right.$ and ${60} \\\\pm {10}\\\\%$ RH) until test.\\n![0197448a-5ae2-7ee0-acc3-abe53a1a126a_53_528_1328_630_483_0.jpg](images/0197448a-5ae2-7ee0-acc3-abe53a1a126a_53_528_1328_630_483_0.jpg)\\nFigure 3-6: OPC concrete specimens cured in lime saturated water curing tank.\\nCapping was done at 26 days in accordance with ASTM C-617 (ASTM standard, 2012b). Forney Hi-cap high strength capping compound was used for capping of the cylinders. If any defects were found in capping then cylinder was recapped before the testing.\\n### 3.6. Test procedure\\n#### 3.6.1. Partic', 'original_tokens': 557, 'general_tokens': 557, 'domain_tokens': 466, 'text_adapted': True, 'tokenizer_used': 'matbert_tokenizer_for_domain'}"}, 'highlights': {'content': ['Forney <mark>Hi</mark>-cap high strength capping compound was used for capping of the cylinders.']}}]
